and they'll keep hunting zebras. So, their numbers will reach 10.000. But that number is insustainable for the zebras. So much Lions will kill s\u00f3 many zebras, that the zebra population will drop from 100 to 20 thousand.<br /> <br /> Now, ALL the Lions will go hungry. There is another catch here: zebras eat grass. That place has enough grass for around 100.000 zebras. But, with so many Lions hunting all the zebras, when their numbers drop to 20.000, now the grass can grow A LOT (so, it will be able to sustain 200.000 zebras... For a while, before the grass gets scarce again). Meanwhile, the Lions are starving. They are dying. Like flies. When enough Lions have died, the number of zebras can grow again. S\u00f3, the 10.000 lions, will drop to only 200. All the 9.800 others, died, their skinny bodies looking like skeletons before the end. A terrible death. A terrible suffering.<br /> <br /> But, with only 200 Lions, all the zebras that haven't been slaughtered can grow unhindered. Without that many predators, their numbers will grow. When their numbers grow, now the Lions have enough prey to survive. The grass will drop too when the zebras grow, so sometimes, the amount of grass can kill both zebras and lions; if there is too much zebras and too few Lions, the grass will disappear, and the zebra will all starve and die, looking like corpses before the end. Terrible death. When their numbers drop, grass can grow again, allowing the surviving zebra to grow once more.<br /> <br /> That's nature's beautiful and magical <i>balance</i>. Nature's balance is an horror show. That balance is achieved by a very simple mechanism - hunger and death. There is nothing pretty about it. <br /> <br /> So, when you suggest that the green revolution was something bad, what your ignorance is saying is that all those 3.5 billion people beyond what would be the &quot;natural&quot; growth in that period, that all those should've <b><u>DIED OF HUNGER, AND AMONGST THOSE, AT LEAST 75% SHOUL